---
title: Remote functions
description: What SvelteKit remote functions mean for Formsnap today, which parts are not supported yet, and the version facts to check before you copy any example.
---

<script>
	import { Callout } from "$lib/markdown";
</script>

SvelteKit's [remote functions](https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/remote-functions) are experimental and opt-in. Formsnap does not integrate with remote **forms** yet. This page records the current boundary so you can plan around it rather than guess from examples written for a different version.

Forsnap presents field state that Superforms owns. A native remote form owns its own field state, so the two cannot simply be combined on the same `form` prop.

## Support matrix

| Usage                                                                            | Status                    | Who owns the state                                                  |
| -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Remote `query` / `prerender` loading data beside an ordinary Formsnap form        | Works as-is               | SvelteKit loads the data; Superforms keeps the form                  |
| Submitting a Superforms form through a remote `command`                           | Application-owned         | Superforms keeps the form; your code translates the command's result into its stores |
| A native remote `form` rendered with Kit's own `fields.as(...)` and `issues()`     | Works, without Formsnap   | Kit owns values, issues and submission                               |
| A native remote `form` presented by Formsnap's `Field` / `Control` components      | **Not supported yet**     | Intended split: Kit owns state, Formsnap owns presentation           |

## Why a native remote form is not a drop-in

`Field`, `Fieldset`, `ElementField` and `useFormField` read four Superforms stores: `form`, `errors`, `constraints` and `tainted`. A native remote form has no such stores. It exposes `fields`, `issues()`, `allIssues()`, `result`, `pending` and a form attachment instead.

Passing a remote form as the `form` prop is therefore not supported, and the wider `SuperForm` contract that `useFormField` exposes cannot be faked for it without inventing lifecycle members that do not exist.

<Callout type="warning">
	Do not build a stand-in object with dummy Superforms methods to make the types line up. The field state would be wrong at runtime, and the failure appears as missing errors rather than a type error.
</Callout>

## Submitting through a remote command

Calling a remote `command` from your own submit handler keeps Superforms and Formsnap in charge of the form and only swaps the mutation mechanism.

This is an application-level integration, not something Formsnap provides, and it is JavaScript-dependent: a command has no native HTML form contract, so there is no progressive enhancement unless you also implement a real action or endpoint as a fallback. Server validation and authorization remain your responsibility in every case.

The version facts below were established by building this integration against the installed versions, and the fixture app in this repository runs it end to end at `/recipes/remote-command`. The route that presents a **native remote form** with Formsnap is a different thing entirely: that one is a presentation boundary, and it is the row marked *not supported yet*, not this one.

- **`customRequest`, not SPA mode.** Superforms' SPA mode cancels the submission and runs client validation instead, so a request never happens and a registered custom request is never reached. A command submission therefore registers `customRequest` from the form's `onSubmit`, which is also where the SPA documentation points for your own request handling.
- **`customRequest` may resolve an `ActionResult`.** The released 3.x source accepts `Response`, `XMLHttpRequest` or an `ActionResult` directly, although the public events page still shows only the first two. What the stores read is `data.form`: it has to be a `SuperValidated`-shaped object whose `id` matches the form's, with `valid`, `errors`, `data` and `message`. A `success` result makes Superforms reset the form when `resetForm` is on, a `failure` result applies the errors and keeps what you put in `data`, and both need `applyAction` to be `false` unless you really have page data to apply.
- **The default `onError` throws.** A result of type `error` reaches Superforms' `onError`, whose default throws, and a throw from there aborts the submission before its pending state is finalised: the form stays busy. Handle it, or translate a failed request into one of the result types above.
- **A command's schema failure is not a field issue.** When the command's own schema rejects its input, the call rejects rather than returning issues, so there is nothing to map onto controls; present it as a failed request.
- **No `redirect()`.** A command cannot redirect inside its handler, so return something the client can navigate with and let the application do the navigation.

## Version facts to check before copying an example

Remote functions changed between SvelteKit 2.27 and 3.x, and the published documentation lags the released code in places:

- **Opt-in flags.** Remote functions need `experimental.remoteFunctions` on the `sveltekit()` Vite plugin. `compilerOptions.experimental.async` is only needed when a template uses `await` (queries), not for a plain remote form.
- **Validation options.** SvelteKit 3 uses `validate({ all, preflightOnly })`. The live guide still shows `includeUntouched`, which the released runtime ignores.
- **Input names belong to Kit.** Generated names carry coercion prefixes (`n:` for numbers, `b:` for scalar booleans), an `[]` suffix for arrays, and a `/<form-id>` suffix that the server requires. Never replace a generated name with the logical field name; the server rejects the submission.
- **Rejection status.** A native remote-form submission returns `200` and renders the issues into the page, even when the data is invalid. Classic enhanced actions behave differently: since SvelteKit 3 they return the status passed to `fail(...)`, where they previously always returned `200`. Do not copy one expectation onto the other.
- **Sensitive fields.** A field whose name starts with `_` is not echoed back when a native submission is rejected, so it cannot be repopulated from the server response.

## Track upstream work

- [svecosystem/formsnap#228](https://github.com/svecosystem/formsnap/issues/228) tracks remote-function support for Formsnap. That repository is the **upstream** project, which has not been maintained since April 2025, and it is not the package you installed.
- [ciscoheat/sveltekit-superforms#616](https://github.com/ciscoheat/sveltekit-superforms/issues/616) tracks the same question for Superforms.

Superforms 3 is a SvelteKit 3 **compatibility** release. It does not add remote-function support, so `sveltekit-superforms` resolving against SvelteKit 3 says nothing about native remote forms working with Formsnap.

This page describes what ships in `@emmorts/formsnap` today. It does not describe unreleased work as available.
